   "line": [
    "c6"
   ],
   "spoken": "A cluster of technique here. Instead of shackling a rook to defend a pawn, remember you can intercept the attackers — a rook to e7 both guards the pawn and prepares to double, better still. Double and trade on your own terms. And before committing, ask the honest question — what happens if I just do it anyway? Here that reveals the killer: with a pawn already on g5, the opponent's bishop has no retreat, so a pawn thrust to g4 traps it outright. That's pure pattern recognition — the very trap a young world-champion-to-be once famously missed in an Alekhine, earning himself the nickname of that move. Studying such games is how the shot becomes instinctive.",
